diagnosis_text:
Full diagnostic sweep — Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot), Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot), and Phase D (pressure_sweep + dhcp_link_history) — returned no confirmed fault on any device or path.

The Phase 1 reachability baseline shows pc0↔pc12 at 0% loss in both directions (RTT ~1.4 ms), directly contradicting the helpdesk ticket. Three OSPF signals flagged by ospf_snapshot were investigated to resolution:

(1) r8 "orphaned area 0.0.0.2" — r8 is the sole router in area 0.0.0.2, a deliberate single-router stub. Its virtual link to r4 is correctly configured on both sides: r4 uses `area 1 virtual-link 18.1.4.1` (r8's highest loopback/router-id) and r8 uses `area 1 virtual-link 14.1.4.1` (r4's highest loopback/router-id, confirmed present in r1's OSPF RIB at /32 cost 20). r8's loopbacks (18.1.x.1) appear in r1's routing table at cost 40, proving the virtual link carries backbone LSAs correctly.

(2) r12 carries no OSPF — by design. r12 runs EIGRP 100, advertising 120.0.0.0/8 (pc12's subnet) and holding a kernel default route via 112.1.1.1 (r11). Routing is fully functional.

(3) r11 is the EIGRP↔OSPF redistribution bridge: it runs EIGRP on 112.0.0.0/8 (toward r12/r13) and redistributes both connected and EIGRP routes into OSPF area 3, from which r1 distributes them backbone-wide. No configuration gap exists.

The reported claim ("since last night's maintenance, every ping vanishes") has no corroborating mechanism: no flap script or process, no carrier transitions, no DHCP/link history events, and the current forwarding state is completely healthy. Per the diagnostic protocol, the claim is unconfirmed.
=== END ===